lowering:
New IR ops — there is nothing to desugar to (no existing IR primitive computes a transcendental). Add three unary f64->f64 ops: SinF64, CosF64, TanF64. pi() is the only pure-frontend piece: fold it to the f64 literal with bits 0x400921FB54442D18 (== Rust std::f64::consts::PI == Python math.pi, measured bit-equal to DuckDB). Integer/FLOAT/DECIMAL arguments reuse the existing cast-to-f64 op in front (round-nearest; i64::MAX -> 9.223372036854776e18, measured).

Rust semantics (identical shape for all three; only the libm call differs):

    #[inline]
    fn duck_sin(x: f64) -> Result<f64, EngineError> {
        if x.is_nan() {
            return Ok(x); // MUST return the input bits untouched — DuckDB preserves
                          // NaN payload+sign exactly (even sNaN 0x7ff0deadbeef0001);
                          // calling libm here would quiet the payload and diverge.
        }
        if x.is_infinite() {
            return Err(EngineError::out_of_range(format!(
                "input value {} is out of range for numeric function",
                if x > 0.0 { "inf" } else { "-inf" },
            ))); // per-row runtime trap, mirrors DuckDB OutOfRangeException
        }
        Ok(x.sin()) // platform libm; 10k-sample differential vs DuckDB: 0 bit mismatches
    }

Trap condition: ONLY +-inf. NaN is a non-trap passthrough; every finite double (incl. denormals, DBL_MAX, 1e300) is a plain libm call. NULL propagation is the generic unary null mask — and note rows removed by WHERE must never evaluate the op (measured: an inf row filtered out does not trap), so the JIT must not speculatively execute trig over inactive lanes.

Cranelift backend: there is no native sin/cos/tan instruction — emit a call to the same extern "C" wrapper functions the interpreter uses (nan-check + inf-trap + libm), NOT a bare libm call, so both backends share one symbol and NaN-payload/trap behavior is identical by construction.

Binder-level rules the frontend must enforce: sin/cos/tan accept all numeric types (any int width, FLOAT, DOUBLE, DECIMAL) -> DOUBLE; VARCHAR columns and BOOLEAN are binder errors (no implicit cast), while string LITERALS are cast at bind time ('1.5' works, 'abc' is a bind-time conversion error); arity is fixed (sin(), sin(1,2), pi(1) are binder errors).
